The cooking window tells you which dish you get, but not how good it will be. Project Cook adds the numbers. Each dish card of the "THIS POT" panel shows one line for each quality level that can occur, with the chance of that level and what the dish gives at that level. So you can see if one more seasoning or a better ingredient is worth it, before the ingredients are gone.

With the default settings, Project Cook does not change cooking results, recipes, or what you get from food. The previews and food sort change what the windows show. The Cooking tag is kept in your save and lets you cook from more home storages.

[size=5]Features[/size]
[list]
[*][b]Dish previews.[/b] See quality chances, eat values, and portion counts before cooking, including drinks from the Juicer and Coffee Machine.[/*]
[*][b]Cooking tooltips.[/b] Hover over dish cards for tier, cooking XP, and trade values. Ingredient tooltips show raw stats and trade values, with tier badges on ingredients.[/*]
[*][b]Food sort.[/b] Find food by Satiety, Morale, Stamina, Life, Trade value, or Expiration Date, with numbers on each item.[/*]
[*][b]Cook from home storages.[/b] Use ingredients directly from cabinets and other home storages in the cooking window, including the Juicer and Coffee Machine. Add the Cooking tag to a storage, or use its existing Food tag. Available from cooking level 2.[/*]
[/list]

Supported languages: English and Chinese.

Eat values include fixed talents and the difficulty's Life factor, but temporary buffs or talent effects, random talent bonuses, being tired of the food, and stat maximums can change the actual gain.

Trade values include your appraisal talents. They exclude the trader's bonus for wanted items and any reduction to half value.

[size=5]Using the food sort[/size]
Choose a sort from the dropdown below the grid. Each item shows the selected number, including ingredients on the cooking workbench:
[list]
[*][b]Satiety, Morale, Stamina, Life:[/b] eat value per use, highest first. Positive values are green and negative values are red.[/*]
[*][b]Trade value:[/b] value of the whole stack, highest first, in gold.[/*]
[*][b]Expiration Date:[/b] rotten food first, then expired food, then food with the fewest days left. White numbers show days until expiration; red numbers on expired food show days until it spoils. Food that cannot spoil further shows Expired or Rotten instead of a countdown.[/*]
[*]Items with no value for the selected choice come last and are dimmed.[/*]
[/list]
The food sort changes only the displayed order. One choice applies to both windows until you quit the game. Select Default to see the items in their stored places and rearrange them by hand. Transfers between grids still work while sorted, and Auto Organize still moves items as usual. If the items cannot fit in the sorted order, the grid shows their stored places.

[size=5]How the ingredient tier works[/size]
[b]Quality and tier are separate.[/b] Quality is the cooking result: Perfect, Good, Average, or Failed. Tier is High-end, Mid-tier, or Low-grade and matters for recipes that use ingredient types, such as Meat + Vegetables. A High-end dish can still turn out Failed, and a Low-grade dish can turn out Perfect. Project Cook shows the dish tier and the chance of each quality.
[list]
[*]The tier matters only for a recipe that asks for ingredient types (for example Meat + Vegetables). A recipe that asks for specific items has fixed stats and ignores the tier.[/*]
[*]The dish gets the tier of the highest-tier ingredient used by the recipe. One High-end ingredient is enough; more of them add nothing to the tier. Cooking talents can also raise the dish tier.[/*]
[*]The tier multiplies the ingredient totals for satiety, health, and life: High-end x1.7, Mid-tier x1.4, Low-grade x1.1. Quality applies another multiplier, and satiety also gets a flat bonus per ingredient. The tier also selects the dish (for example Eight-Treasure Stew, Stew, or Simple Stew), and with it the base trade value.[/*]
[*]The multiplier works on the sum of the ingredient stats. A High-end ingredient with weak stats (for example Enoki Mushrooms) can raise the tier and the trade value, and still lower the stats. The dish card shows the final numbers, so compare there.[/*]
[*]The tier of an ingredient comes from its price. Staple food, snacks, and drinks have no tier.[/*]
[/list]

[size=5]Limitations[/size]
[list]
[*]Cooking previews are not available in hot pot mode.[/*]
[/list]

[size=5]Requirements[/size]
[list]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/survivallog/mods/12]BepInEx for Survival Log[/url]. Install it first.[/*]
[/list]

[size=5]Installation[/size]
[list=1]
[*]Install [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/survivallog/mods/12]BepInEx[/url] [i](if no other mods were installed before, start the game once so BepInEx finishes setup, then quit)[/i].[/*]
[*]Extract this mod's zip into the game folder (the folder with the game .exe). The DLL lands in BepInEx\plugins. Full path example:
[code]C:\Program Files (x86)\Steam\steamapps\common\Survival Log\BepInEx\plugins\ProjectCook.dll[/code]
[/*]
[/list]

[size=5]Uninstall[/size]
Delete [font=Courier New]ProjectCook.dll[/font] from the [font=Courier New]BepInEx\plugins[/font] folder. The game then drops the Cooking tag. A storage with only the Cooking tag loses its rule, so give it a game tag (for example Food) before you uninstall.

[size=5]Configuration[/size]
The config file [font=Courier New]BepInEx\config\com.ivmakk.survivallog.projectcook.cfg[/font] is created the first time you start the game with the mod. Dish preview, food sort, and cooking storages are all on by default and can be turned off separately. Edit the file while the game is closed; changes take effect on the next start. See the [url=https://github.com/ivmakk-sl/sl-project-cook/blob/main/CONFIG.md]settings reference[/url] for the switches and their effects.

Before turning off [font=Courier New]CookingStorages[/font], give any storage with only Cooking a game tag such as Food. Turning this feature off removes Cooking tags from the save, just like uninstalling; turning it back on does not restore them.

